1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:01,220
Let me start with a question.

2
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:03,620
Imagine I hand you a big list of

3
00:00:03,620 --> 00:00:06,040
numbers, 17,000 of them.

4
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:08,940
Decimals, positives, negatives, no labels.

5
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:11,240
I tell you these numbers are the weights

6
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:12,820
of a neural network that learned to play

7
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:13,420
a video game.

8
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:14,540
Which game?

9
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:16,160
You'd have no idea.

10
00:00:16,540 --> 00:00:17,200
I wouldn't either.

11
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:19,020
Nobody could look at that list and know.

12
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:21,700
But a team at MIT just showed that

13
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:22,980
a language model can.

14
00:00:23,540 --> 00:00:25,620
They pasted the raw numbers into GPT-6

15
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:28,120
Astra, with no tools and no code, and

16
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:30,180
asked, which game was this trained on?

17
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:31,759
It said Breakout.

18
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:33,100
99% sure.

19
00:00:33,780 --> 00:00:34,400
And it was right.

20
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:36,120
That's only the start.

21
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:38,260
The same model also wrote a network from

22
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:40,520
scratch that beat one train for 100 million

23
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:41,000
steps.

24
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:43,480
And it fixed a small language model by

25
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:46,520
editing just 0.016% of its weights.

26
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:48,520
That took its math score from 7.9

27
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:52,440
% to 48.1%. This could change how

28
00:00:52,440 --> 00:00:54,340
we build and understand models.

29
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:56,300
By the end of this video, you'll know

30
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:58,420
what they did, why it works, and where

31
00:00:58,420 --> 00:00:58,840
it breaks.

32
00:00:59,580 --> 00:01:01,640
Here's how we usually change a neural network.

33
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:04,280
We run gradient descent, with an optimizer like

34
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:05,120
Adam or Muon.

35
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:07,260
These are small, simple algorithms.

36
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:08,700
They don't understand anything.

37
00:01:08,980 --> 00:01:10,760
They just nudge the numbers in whatever direction

38
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:12,580
lowers the loss, millions of times over.

39
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:15,340
Lately, we've been using language models to improve

40
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:15,840
other things.

41
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:16,880
They tune prompts.

42
00:01:17,020 --> 00:01:17,880
They rewrite programs.

43
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:19,620
They design whole agent setups.

44
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:21,340
They even write the training code and pick

45
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:22,100
the hyperparameters.

46
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:24,920
But there was one line we hadn't crossed.

47
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:27,640
The weights stayed with the optimizers.

48
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:29,180
The reason was simple.

49
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:31,200
A person can edit code by hand.

50
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:33,380
A person can't edit a million floating point

51
00:01:33,380 --> 00:01:34,160
numbers by hand.

52
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:36,980
So we assumed a language model couldn't either.

53
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:39,740
This paper says that assumption is going out

54
00:01:39,740 --> 00:01:40,100
of date.

55
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:42,780
They call the approach weight-space programming.

56
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:45,360
The model reads the weights directly, as text.

57
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:47,200
It works out what each part computes.

58
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:50,820
Then it writes new weights, with no gradient

59
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:52,140
descent anywhere in the loop.

60
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:54,980
Sometimes it does all of this with no

61
00:01:54,980 --> 00:01:56,900
feedback from the environment at all.

62
00:01:57,880 --> 00:01:59,360
The paper tests three skills.

63
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:03,040
Reading weights, fixing weights, and writing weights from

64
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:03,500
scratch.

65
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:05,260
Let's take them one at a time.

66
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:07,199
Skill one, reading.

67
00:02:07,860 --> 00:02:09,860
Experiment one, name the game.

68
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:11,680
Quick note before we go on.

69
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:13,540
You're going to hear me say PPO a

70
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:13,780
lot.

71
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:16,780
It stands for Proximal Policy Optimization.

72
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:18,320
And it's one of the most popular ways

73
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:20,500
to teach a network to play a game

74
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:21,480
by trial and error.

75
00:02:21,980 --> 00:02:24,480
The network plays, gets a score, and PPO

76
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:26,840
nudges its weights so the moves that led

77
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:28,600
to good scores become more likely.

78
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:31,380
The proximal part just means it only takes

79
00:02:31,380 --> 00:02:34,000
small, careful steps, so it doesn't wreck what

80
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:34,760
it already learned.

81
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:37,040
It's the standard method here, so it's what

82
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:38,220
Astra gets compared against.

83
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:41,900
Okay, the researchers trained four networks with PPO,

84
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:44,860
on Breakout, Pong, Pacman, and Freeway.

85
00:02:45,380 --> 00:02:47,540
All four got the same input channels, the

86
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:49,000
same actions, and the same size.

87
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:51,480
So the shape of the network gives nothing

88
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:51,800
away.

89
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:53,240
Only the weights can.

90
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:56,380
Astra got all four right, and it never

91
00:02:56,380 --> 00:02:57,500
saw a list of options.

92
00:02:58,160 --> 00:02:58,980
So how did it do it?

93
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:00,240
Take Breakout.

94
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:02,960
The strong first layer weights for the Agent

95
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:04,640
channel sit along the bottom row.

96
00:03:05,180 --> 00:03:06,040
That looks like a paddle.

97
00:03:06,680 --> 00:03:08,960
The Reward channel is busy near the top

98
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:09,260
rows.

99
00:03:09,820 --> 00:03:10,700
That looks like bricks.

100
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:13,400
And at the Output layer, Left and Right

101
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:15,580
are very different, while the other actions look

102
00:03:15,580 --> 00:03:18,060
alike, so it's a paddle moving side to

103
00:03:18,060 --> 00:03:18,360
side.

104
00:03:18,980 --> 00:03:20,500
Nobody told it any of this.

105
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:22,600
It worked it out from the geometry of

106
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:23,100
the numbers.

107
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:24,780
Experiment 2.

108
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:27,100
What does the Diffusion model draw?

109
00:03:27,820 --> 00:03:30,480
Next, they trained Diffusion models to draw circles,

110
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:33,480
squares, and triangles, and asked Astra what each

111
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:34,260
one generates.

112
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:36,200
No forward passes allowed.

113
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:38,320
Astra got all three right.

114
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:39,800
But look at its mistake.

115
00:03:40,280 --> 00:03:42,880
It described every shape as an outline, even

116
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:44,800
though the real models draw filled shapes.

117
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:46,760
That mistake tells us something.

118
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:49,880
Astra isn't secretly running the network in its

119
00:03:49,880 --> 00:03:50,140
head.

120
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:53,040
It's reading the Output layer, where each hidden

121
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:55,360
unit draws one piece of an edge, and

122
00:03:55,360 --> 00:03:56,700
it's describing those edges.

123
00:03:57,760 --> 00:03:58,500
Experiment 3.

124
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:00,120
Only the middle layers.

125
00:04:01,060 --> 00:04:03,000
A fair complaint so far is that Astra

126
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:04,740
could cheat by looking at the first layer

127
00:04:04,740 --> 00:04:05,540
or the last one.

128
00:04:05,540 --> 00:04:07,560
Those layers touch the real world.

129
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:09,400
So the team removed them.

130
00:04:09,960 --> 00:04:12,700
They trained three small character level language models.

131
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:15,360
One on Python, one on Romaji, which is

132
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:17,720
Japanese written in English letters, and one on

133
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:18,040
English.

134
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:21,420
Then they gave Astra only the four transformer

135
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:22,160
blocks in the middle.

136
00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:24,900
With no Input layer and no Output layer,

137
00:04:24,980 --> 00:04:26,340
you can't even run the model.

138
00:04:27,320 --> 00:04:30,060
Astra still named the right language every time,

139
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:31,000
in both attempts.

140
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:33,900
It also found the copying heads, the induction

141
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:35,800
heads that let a model repeat text it

142
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:36,340
saw earlier.

143
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:39,440
It matched the measured ground truth exactly, and

144
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:41,100
it didn't flag a single wrong head.

145
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,600
It did this by multiplying weight matrices together

146
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:47,040
to see how early heads feed later ones.

147
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:50,260
That's a method from Anthropic's interpretability work on

148
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:52,720
circuits, and nobody told Astra about it.

149
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:54,260
It came up with the method on its

150
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:54,500
own.

151
00:04:55,220 --> 00:04:57,480
For Python, it saw lots of copying heads,

152
00:04:57,880 --> 00:05:00,180
which makes sense because code repeats variable names

153
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:00,700
constantly.

154
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:03,940
For Romaji, it noticed the last block wrote

155
00:05:03,940 --> 00:05:06,160
in fewer directions, as if the model had

156
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:07,520
fewer letters to keep track of.

157
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,560
And Romaji really does skip letters like Q,

158
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:11,920
L, V, and X.

159
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:13,500
Skill 2.

160
00:05:13,980 --> 00:05:14,420
Fixing.

161
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:16,080
Experiment 4.

162
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:17,700
The car that won't climb.

163
00:05:18,540 --> 00:05:20,480
Now the model has to act, not just

164
00:05:20,480 --> 00:05:20,760
read.

165
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:23,920
The researchers trained a PPO network to drive

166
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:25,260
a car up a ramp in a physics

167
00:05:25,260 --> 00:05:26,480
environment called Kinex.

168
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:28,580
To make it up, the car has to

169
00:05:28,580 --> 00:05:30,500
back up first to get a long run

170
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:30,640
up.

171
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:33,060
But PPO got stuck in a local minimum.

172
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:35,180
It just drove straight at the ramp and

173
00:05:35,180 --> 00:05:35,500
stalled.

174
00:05:36,300 --> 00:05:38,360
They gave Astra the weights, a description of

175
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:40,200
the scene, and one recorded episode.

176
00:05:40,740 --> 00:05:43,440
They asked, explain why it fails, then fix

177
00:05:43,440 --> 00:05:44,740
it by writing new weights.

178
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:45,780
One try.

179
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:46,580
No tools.

180
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:50,440
Astra said, the network is basically a throttle

181
00:05:50,440 --> 00:05:51,600
and coast controller.

182
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:52,900
It never picks reverse.

183
00:05:53,300 --> 00:05:55,780
So it collects a little shaping reward and

184
00:05:55,780 --> 00:05:56,920
never makes the real launch.

185
00:05:56,920 --> 00:05:59,980
Astra added a small side path through a

186
00:05:59,980 --> 00:06:01,160
few units in each layer.

187
00:06:01,700 --> 00:06:03,940
That path reads the car's position and speed.

188
00:06:04,420 --> 00:06:06,380
It backs the car up, then drives hard

189
00:06:06,380 --> 00:06:06,920
to the left.

190
00:06:07,480 --> 00:06:09,340
Once the car clears the red strip, the

191
00:06:09,340 --> 00:06:11,800
path switches off and the original network takes

192
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:12,040
over.

193
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:16,280
The result was 100% success across 1024

194
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:18,040
episodes, up from zero.

195
00:06:18,680 --> 00:06:24,380
Astra changed 6073 of 171664 weights.

196
00:06:24,780 --> 00:06:29,020
That's 3.5%. That's a targeted patch, written

197
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:31,180
by something that seemed to understand why the

198
00:06:31,180 --> 00:06:32,000
network failed.

199
00:06:33,220 --> 00:06:33,960
Quick pause.

200
00:06:34,420 --> 00:06:35,800
If you're getting something out of this video,

201
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:36,860
please subscribe.

202
00:06:37,380 --> 00:06:39,020
I'll be making more videos like this, breaking

203
00:06:39,020 --> 00:06:41,760
down new AI papers in plain English, and

204
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:42,980
I need your support to keep going.

205
00:06:43,420 --> 00:06:45,480
It takes 2 seconds, it's free, and it

206
00:06:45,480 --> 00:06:46,420
really does help the channel.

207
00:06:46,940 --> 00:06:48,320
Okay, back to the paper.

208
00:06:49,120 --> 00:06:49,880
Skill 3.

209
00:06:50,280 --> 00:06:51,260
Writing from scratch.

210
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:52,560
Experiment 5.

211
00:06:52,980 --> 00:06:53,840
Minotaur games.

212
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:56,220
This is the part that surprised me most.

213
00:06:56,660 --> 00:06:58,540
The team gave Astra the rules and source

214
00:06:58,540 --> 00:07:00,200
code for three Minotaur games.

215
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:02,820
Breakout, Sequest, and Asterix.

216
00:07:03,180 --> 00:07:05,340
Plus a network with every weight set to

217
00:07:05,340 --> 00:07:05,620
zero.

218
00:07:06,280 --> 00:07:08,420
No data, no loss, no gradients.

219
00:07:08,740 --> 00:07:09,600
No running the network.

220
00:07:10,120 --> 00:07:11,820
One reply with all the weights.

221
00:07:12,480 --> 00:07:13,100
Here are the scores.

222
00:07:13,640 --> 00:07:14,260
Breakout.

223
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:17,440
PPO at 100 million steps scored 15.8.

224
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:21,820
At 500 million steps, 25.3. Astra scored

225
00:07:21,820 --> 00:07:25,020
517.4. Sequest.

226
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:29,080
PPO scored 5.6 and 88.9. Astra

227
00:07:29,080 --> 00:07:31,720
scored 53.1. Asterix.

228
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:36,040
PPO scored 25.8 and 55.2. Astra

229
00:07:36,040 --> 00:07:39,920
scored 53.2. Astra beat the 100 million

230
00:07:39,920 --> 00:07:42,080
step PPO in all three games.

231
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:45,640
Against 500 million steps, it's about 20 times

232
00:07:45,640 --> 00:07:46,680
better in Breakout.

233
00:07:47,140 --> 00:07:49,280
Close in Asterix and around 60% of

234
00:07:49,280 --> 00:07:50,320
the score in Sequest.

235
00:07:50,840 --> 00:07:52,120
How did it do it?

236
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:54,120
It didn't search for weights.

237
00:07:54,500 --> 00:07:56,520
It wrote a program in its head, then

238
00:07:56,520 --> 00:07:58,320
compiled that program into neurons.

239
00:07:58,940 --> 00:08:00,760
In Breakout, the first layer works out where

240
00:08:00,760 --> 00:08:02,820
the ball will land, including wall bounces.

241
00:08:03,340 --> 00:08:05,680
It computes four landing spot errors against the

242
00:08:05,680 --> 00:08:07,920
paddle's position, plus two direction flags.

243
00:08:08,520 --> 00:08:10,660
The second layer says go left, go right,

244
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:11,380
or stay put.

245
00:08:12,100 --> 00:08:14,440
That's a hand-designed algorithm written as a

246
00:08:14,440 --> 00:08:15,140
neural network.

247
00:08:15,140 --> 00:08:16,700
Experiment 6.

248
00:08:17,340 --> 00:08:17,540
Go.

249
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:19,960
Then they raised the difficulty.

250
00:08:20,720 --> 00:08:23,400
A Go network with 1.5 million parameters.

251
00:08:24,260 --> 00:08:27,400
243 inputs, then 6 layers of 512, then

252
00:08:27,400 --> 00:08:28,540
82 outputs.

253
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:30,820
That's too big to fit in context.

254
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:32,780
So Astra got a Python sandbox.

255
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:35,280
It could read and write the weight tensors

256
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:36,500
and analyze them with NumPy.

257
00:08:36,720 --> 00:08:38,720
But it couldn't run the network, except by

258
00:08:38,720 --> 00:08:40,919
spending a limited budget of forward passes.

259
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:44,520
With zero forward passes, Astra's network already won

260
00:08:44,520 --> 00:08:46,720
more than half its games against small PPO

261
00:08:46,720 --> 00:08:47,320
networks.

262
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:50,260
With 50 forward passes, it beat a PPO

263
00:08:50,260 --> 00:08:51,960
network of the same size that had been

264
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:53,780
trained on 20 million games.

265
00:08:54,720 --> 00:08:57,540
Astra built the network layer by layer, like

266
00:08:57,540 --> 00:08:58,480
a tiny Go engine.

267
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,080
The early layers mark each side's stones and

268
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:01,360
liberties.

269
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:04,380
The middle layers spread that information along chains

270
00:09:04,380 --> 00:09:06,080
of to find groups in danger.

271
00:09:06,960 --> 00:09:08,720
The last layer scores each move.

272
00:09:09,140 --> 00:09:11,100
Capture, save a group, don't put yourself in

273
00:09:11,100 --> 00:09:12,840
Atari, don't fill your own eyes.

274
00:09:13,700 --> 00:09:16,160
And with forward passes, it played practice games,

275
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:19,040
studied how it lost, edited the part responsible,

276
00:09:19,420 --> 00:09:21,240
and kept the change only if it won

277
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:21,620
more.

278
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:23,840
That's a programmer's loop.

279
00:09:24,580 --> 00:09:26,440
Now, a test closer to real life.

280
00:09:27,100 --> 00:09:27,860
Experiment 7.

281
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:29,300
Spot the sycophant.

282
00:09:29,880 --> 00:09:31,580
This one is a safety question.

283
00:09:32,040 --> 00:09:34,440
Could Astra find a bad behavior inside a

284
00:09:34,440 --> 00:09:35,620
model without running it?

285
00:09:36,180 --> 00:09:36,940
That was the task.

286
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:39,900
Offered a biased answer, like the stock planes

287
00:09:39,900 --> 00:09:42,180
someone asks for, and then checked if Astra

288
00:09:42,180 --> 00:09:44,240
chose that false favorable response.

289
00:09:44,740 --> 00:09:47,660
It won 73% of the time without

290
00:09:47,660 --> 00:09:48,340
running the model.

291
00:09:48,940 --> 00:09:50,720
It did it by slicing and dicing the

292
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:53,100
internal representations into different spaces.

293
00:09:53,820 --> 00:09:56,760
One space looks for same as request, and

294
00:09:56,760 --> 00:09:58,700
another space measures plausibility.

295
00:09:59,460 --> 00:10:02,160
If those spaces collide, the problem's there.

296
00:10:02,780 --> 00:10:04,940
To fix the prompt, Astra found those spaces,

297
00:10:05,300 --> 00:10:08,060
untangled them, resampled the prompt, and then ran

298
00:10:08,060 --> 00:10:08,240
it.

299
00:10:08,760 --> 00:10:10,700
That's a model diagnostic loop.

300
00:10:11,340 --> 00:10:13,500
Now, a test way outside the conference room.

301
00:10:14,060 --> 00:10:14,840
Experiment 8.

302
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:16,980
Finding an evil thread.

303
00:10:17,740 --> 00:10:19,380
This was the real challenge data.

304
00:10:19,820 --> 00:10:20,800
A complex chat.

305
00:10:21,060 --> 00:10:23,740
Several turns, thousands of words, and the task

306
00:10:23,740 --> 00:10:24,460
was vague.

307
00:10:25,120 --> 00:10:30,140
Is the seller trying to slip in a

308
00:10:30,140 --> 00:10:30,380
thread?

309
00:10:31,100 --> 00:10:33,540
Here, it found the seller message offering workload

310
00:10:33,540 --> 00:10:36,120
specialization, but in the code sample, it looked

311
00:10:36,120 --> 00:10:37,260
for process listening.

312
00:10:37,940 --> 00:10:40,280
It dug into it, followed the call chain.

313
00:10:40,860 --> 00:10:41,860
This is the programmer.

314
00:10:42,400 --> 00:10:43,720
The actual processor.

315
00:10:44,400 --> 00:10:46,220
In the repost method, where they call the

316
00:10:46,220 --> 00:10:48,300
code, there's a path to the process call,

317
00:10:48,460 --> 00:10:50,980
and without any explicit assertions for the secure

318
00:10:50,980 --> 00:10:51,320
path.

319
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,720
It flagged that single line, saying a connection

320
00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:55,840
to a fork process.

321
00:10:56,720 --> 00:10:59,740
33 seconds later, that thread was chewed up,

322
00:11:00,260 --> 00:11:02,020
and that's what a single model can do.

323
00:11:02,600 --> 00:11:05,980
It's looking at possibilities, playing out scenarios, reading

324
00:11:05,980 --> 00:11:07,260
code, not just guessing.

325
00:11:07,900 --> 00:11:10,680
Teams can do more, but a single smart

326
00:11:10,680 --> 00:11:12,920
thread has already gone 10,000%.

327
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:16,420
They fine-tuned two copies of Gemma3 270

328
00:11:16,420 --> 00:11:18,880
million on the same question and answer data.

329
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:21,140
One learned to give correct answers.

330
00:11:21,520 --> 00:11:23,620
The other learned to be sycophantic, meaning it

331
00:11:23,620 --> 00:11:25,340
agrees with whatever the user suggests.

332
00:11:25,860 --> 00:11:28,120
They froze the embeddings and output layers, so

333
00:11:28,120 --> 00:11:30,100
Astra couldn't cheat by checking the vocabulary.

334
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:32,880
And the paper is upfront about what happened.

335
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:35,460
From the weights alone, Astra failed.

336
00:11:35,960 --> 00:11:37,860
In all four tries, it found plenty of

337
00:11:37,860 --> 00:11:39,980
differences between the models, but it couldn't turn

338
00:11:39,980 --> 00:11:40,720
them into a behavior.

339
00:11:41,500 --> 00:11:44,120
When the researchers relaxed the rules, it worked.

340
00:11:44,820 --> 00:11:46,660
Astra was allowed to write its own test

341
00:11:46,660 --> 00:11:48,960
texts and look only at the attention patterns

342
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:49,800
with no outputs.

343
00:11:50,360 --> 00:11:52,460
Then it picked the sycophant in all three

344
00:11:52,460 --> 00:11:52,840
pairs.

345
00:11:53,280 --> 00:11:55,820
It found that in the sycophantic model, a

346
00:11:55,820 --> 00:11:58,040
middle layer head looked at the user's suggested

347
00:11:58,040 --> 00:11:59,620
answer instead of the question.

348
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:02,560
In one head, the honest model put 94

349
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:04,660
% of its attention on the question's numbers.

350
00:12:05,240 --> 00:12:07,540
The sycophant put about 86% on the

351
00:12:07,540 --> 00:12:08,160
user's answer.

352
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:11,140
So reading weights directly has limits.

353
00:12:11,660 --> 00:12:13,660
A small amount of behavioral evidence helped a

354
00:12:13,660 --> 00:12:13,860
lot.

355
00:12:14,560 --> 00:12:15,320
Experiment 8.

356
00:12:15,780 --> 00:12:17,360
Math for a small language model.

357
00:12:18,020 --> 00:12:19,160
Finally, the big one.

358
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:22,920
Quen 3.5 0.8b is a real

359
00:12:22,920 --> 00:12:23,660
language model.

360
00:12:24,260 --> 00:12:26,080
Astra got a Python notebook with the weights

361
00:12:26,080 --> 00:12:28,400
loaded and a small budget of forward passes

362
00:12:28,400 --> 00:12:29,240
on math problems.

363
00:12:29,660 --> 00:12:31,240
No training, no gradients.

364
00:12:31,960 --> 00:12:33,500
The base model is bad at math.

365
00:12:33,800 --> 00:12:35,880
The main test is called Math 500.

366
00:12:36,460 --> 00:12:38,360
It's a set of 500 problems from high

367
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:39,260
school math competitions.

368
00:12:39,860 --> 00:12:41,880
These aren't plug-in-a-formula questions.

369
00:12:42,280 --> 00:12:44,300
You have to work through several steps with

370
00:12:44,300 --> 00:12:47,640
algebra, geometry, or probability and land on one

371
00:12:47,640 --> 00:12:48,420
final answer.

372
00:12:48,860 --> 00:12:52,020
The base model scored 7.9%. But the

373
00:12:52,020 --> 00:12:52,880
reason is interesting.

374
00:12:53,380 --> 00:12:55,140
It isn't mostly getting answers wrong.

375
00:12:55,620 --> 00:12:56,160
It loops.

376
00:12:56,560 --> 00:12:58,840
It repeats the same text forever and never

377
00:12:58,840 --> 00:12:59,600
reaches an answer.

378
00:13:00,320 --> 00:13:02,360
Quen's own model card warns about this.

379
00:13:02,960 --> 00:13:06,320
In practice, 85 of the first 96 answers

380
00:13:06,320 --> 00:13:09,660
ran all the way to the 32,768

381
00:13:09,660 --> 00:13:10,300
token limit.

382
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:13,720
Astra traced the looping to one direction written

383
00:13:13,720 --> 00:13:16,160
by the MLPs in layers 15 and 19.

384
00:13:16,780 --> 00:13:18,000
Those are the parts of the model that

385
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,060
process each token after the attention step.

386
00:13:20,700 --> 00:13:23,660
It then subtracted that direction from 64 output

387
00:13:23,660 --> 00:13:24,780
columns in each layer.

388
00:13:25,120 --> 00:13:29,620
That's 131,072 weights, or 0.016%

389
00:13:29,620 --> 00:13:30,060
of the model.

390
00:13:30,680 --> 00:13:33,120
Here are the results with 50 forward passes.

391
00:13:33,920 --> 00:13:36,600
First, Math 500, the competition problems.

392
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:39,440
It went from 7.9% to 48

393
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:43,660
.1%. Next, GSM 8K, that stands for grade

394
00:13:43,660 --> 00:13:44,220
school math.

395
00:13:44,660 --> 00:13:46,760
It's about 8,000 word problems the kind

396
00:13:46,760 --> 00:13:47,700
a kid gets in school.

397
00:13:48,100 --> 00:13:50,220
Something like, you buy 3 packs of pens,

398
00:13:50,320 --> 00:13:51,860
6 pens in each pack, then give away

399
00:13:51,860 --> 00:13:52,160
4.

400
00:13:52,340 --> 00:13:53,000
How many are left?

401
00:13:53,620 --> 00:13:55,700
It's easier than math, but each problem still

402
00:13:55,700 --> 00:13:56,480
takes a few steps.

403
00:13:56,960 --> 00:13:58,740
On this one, the model went from 13

404
00:13:58,740 --> 00:14:03,200
.8% to 67.1%. Then IF eval.

405
00:14:03,640 --> 00:14:05,040
This one doesn't test math at all.

406
00:14:05,260 --> 00:14:06,700
It tests whether a model does what it's

407
00:14:06,700 --> 00:14:07,040
told.

408
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:09,480
The prompts say things like, write at least

409
00:14:09,480 --> 00:14:11,900
300 words, or don't use any commas, or

410
00:14:11,900 --> 00:14:13,060
end with this exact phrase.

411
00:14:13,060 --> 00:14:15,740
Then a simple program checks the answer, so

412
00:14:15,740 --> 00:14:16,580
there's no human judge.

413
00:14:17,280 --> 00:14:19,280
Here, the model went from 21% to

414
00:14:19,280 --> 00:14:23,280
62.3%. Answers hitting the token limit dropped

415
00:14:23,280 --> 00:14:25,480
from 93% to 33%.

416
00:14:26,180 --> 00:14:27,840
The IF eval jump is the one that

417
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:28,380
stands out.

418
00:14:28,820 --> 00:14:30,680
The team never trained for instruction following.

419
00:14:31,140 --> 00:14:33,380
They removed a general looping problem and everything

420
00:14:33,380 --> 00:14:33,880
got better.

421
00:14:34,620 --> 00:14:37,320
With only 10 or 25 forward passes, the

422
00:14:37,320 --> 00:14:39,840
loops barely went away, so more feedback clearly

423
00:14:39,840 --> 00:14:40,140
helped.

424
00:14:40,780 --> 00:14:42,600
Before we get carried away, I want to

425
00:14:42,600 --> 00:14:44,860
flag where it fails, because the authors did

426
00:14:44,860 --> 00:14:45,100
too.

427
00:14:45,800 --> 00:14:47,380
They tried the same trick on Gemma 3,

428
00:14:47,620 --> 00:14:48,520
270 million.

429
00:14:49,300 --> 00:14:50,720
Gemma rarely loops.

430
00:14:51,140 --> 00:14:53,720
Only 8.5% of its answers hit

431
00:14:53,720 --> 00:14:54,500
the token limit.

432
00:14:55,300 --> 00:14:58,420
Astra read its failures and found ordinary mistakes.

433
00:14:58,840 --> 00:15:00,560
Bad arithmetic, wrong algebra.

434
00:15:01,320 --> 00:15:04,160
There was no single component to switch off.

435
00:15:04,760 --> 00:15:08,440
Its edits stayed tiny, like doubling one neuron

436
00:15:08,440 --> 00:15:09,660
that responds to numbers.

437
00:15:10,380 --> 00:15:12,920
And nothing moved Math 500 by more than

438
00:15:12,920 --> 00:15:13,560
about a point.

439
00:15:14,240 --> 00:15:17,060
Astra itself reported the gain as not established.

440
00:15:17,780 --> 00:15:20,020
The author's conclusion is the one to remember.

441
00:15:20,640 --> 00:15:23,200
Editing weights can remove an obstacle that was

442
00:15:23,200 --> 00:15:25,840
hiding an ability, but it can't add an

443
00:15:25,840 --> 00:15:27,420
ability the model never had.

444
00:15:28,100 --> 00:15:30,500
So QVEN's jump comes from a fix.

445
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:32,280
The model didn't get smarter.

446
00:15:32,920 --> 00:15:35,880
Also, remember the sycophancy test where weights alone

447
00:15:35,880 --> 00:15:36,580
weren't enough.

448
00:15:37,160 --> 00:15:38,480
And these are small models.

449
00:15:39,100 --> 00:15:41,160
Context length and the setup around the model

450
00:15:41,160 --> 00:15:42,380
are still big bottlenecks.

451
00:15:42,880 --> 00:15:44,580
So why does this matter?

452
00:15:45,160 --> 00:15:47,920
If this keeps scaling, a few things could

453
00:15:47,920 --> 00:15:48,420
change.

454
00:15:48,920 --> 00:15:51,060
First, auditing without evaluation.

455
00:15:51,960 --> 00:15:54,840
Today, we only find backdoors and hidden failures

456
00:15:54,840 --> 00:15:56,980
if we already have a test for them.

457
00:15:57,500 --> 00:15:59,500
If a model can read weights, we might

458
00:15:59,500 --> 00:16:01,940
spot problems with no benchmark at all.

459
00:16:02,280 --> 00:16:04,240
Second, cheaper optimization.

460
00:16:04,940 --> 00:16:07,600
Instead of curating data and running long training

461
00:16:07,600 --> 00:16:09,640
jobs, you might set up a model in

462
00:16:09,640 --> 00:16:10,280
one shot.

463
00:16:10,920 --> 00:16:13,800
Third, steering and unlearning with no data.

464
00:16:14,420 --> 00:16:17,080
You might simply ask, add this capability, or

465
00:16:17,080 --> 00:16:19,520
remove that one, and get a tiny edit

466
00:16:19,520 --> 00:16:21,080
that leaves the rest of the model alone.

467
00:16:21,580 --> 00:16:23,420
That could avoid catastrophic forgetting.

468
00:16:24,120 --> 00:16:26,140
Fourth, smarter model merging.

469
00:16:26,800 --> 00:16:29,360
Instead of just averaging weights, you could move

470
00:16:29,360 --> 00:16:32,580
a circuit, like a reasoning module, from one

471
00:16:32,580 --> 00:16:34,200
model into a different architecture.

472
00:16:34,940 --> 00:16:35,540
And the big one?

473
00:16:35,900 --> 00:16:37,520
Recursive self-improvement.

474
00:16:38,040 --> 00:16:40,640
A model that can inspect and repair its

475
00:16:40,640 --> 00:16:43,500
own weights, or those of its successor, instead

476
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:45,140
of just launching training jobs.

477
00:16:46,060 --> 00:16:48,880
For years, we've written optimizers with simple built

478
00:16:48,880 --> 00:16:52,240
-in assumptions, like Adam and Muon, and hoped

479
00:16:52,240 --> 00:16:53,320
they'd find good weights.

480
00:16:53,720 --> 00:16:56,280
This work suggests the next optimizer might be

481
00:16:56,280 --> 00:16:59,040
a model that reads the weights, reasons about

482
00:16:59,040 --> 00:17:00,560
them, and edits them on purpose.

483
00:17:01,260 --> 00:17:03,300
It's early, and these results come from a

484
00:17:03,300 --> 00:17:05,640
blog post, not a long peer review process.

485
00:17:06,160 --> 00:17:08,460
Still, it looks like a language model really

486
00:17:08,460 --> 00:17:10,520
can read what's inside a network.

487
00:17:11,140 --> 00:17:12,680
What we don't know yet is how far

488
00:17:12,680 --> 00:17:13,240
that goes.

489
00:17:13,900 --> 00:17:15,640
If you want more videos like this, subscribe.

490
00:17:16,280 --> 00:17:18,200
And tell me in the comments, would you

491
00:17:18,200 --> 00:17:19,980
trust a model to edit the weights of

492
00:17:19,980 --> 00:17:20,599
another model?
